;; A Raspberry Pi 3 as the way into your own network.
;;
;; It joins the tailnet at boot and advertises the LAN it sits on, so
;; everything else there - an ESPHome relay on a garage door, a printer, a
;; NAS - is reachable from a phone without installing anything on those
;; devices and without forwarding anything on the router.
;;
;; Set TS_AUTHKEY and TS_ROUTES in /etc/default/tailscale. Routes are inert
;; until approved once in the admin console, and a route of /32 for the one
;; device you want reached is tighter than the whole /24. Turn off key expiry
;; for the node, or it drops off the tailnet in a few months and the door
;; stops answering.
;;
;; Why this rather than the WireGuard image beside it: WireGuard needs an
;; inbound port, which means a port forward, a dynamic DNS name and a public
;; address to forward from. This needs none of them. The cost is a dependency
;; on a coordination service, and a Go binary where WireGuard is a kernel
;; module - the rootfs goes from 120M to 512M for that reason.
;;
;; No appliance daemons: this board is a route into a network, not a
;; simulated device, and the two have no reason to share an image.

(image raspberrypi3-64-tailscale
  (compose
    image:raspberrypi3-64
    feature:dev-ssh
    feature:tailscale)

  (override
    (value buildroot:BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2_SIZE "512M"))

  (verified-against (buildroot "2025.02.16")))
